\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the study. Graphs from ten synthetic families ($5\le n\le 30$) are reduced to six cheap statistics. A log-linear ridge regression fitted on Monte Carlo cover-time labels maps them to a predicted cover time, which is compared with the generic bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$. Every predictor is scored on held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, using a paired two-level bootstrap over graphs and walks.}}{3}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{3}{Overview of the study. Graphs from ten synthetic families ($5\le n\le 30$) are reduced to six cheap statistics. A log-linear ridge regression fitted on Monte Carlo cover-time labels maps them to a predicted cover time, which is compared with the generic bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$. Every predictor is scored on held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, using a paired two-level bootstrap over graphs and walks}{figure.caption.5}{}}
